[[File:Kingston upon Hull UK 2022 Local Election map.svg|thumb|300px|right|Map of the results of the 2022 Hull council election. [[Labour Party (UK)|Labour]] in red, [[Liberal Democrats (UK)|Liberal Democrats]] in yellow, Uncontested in cream.]]

The '''2022 Hull City Council election''' took place on 5 May 2022 to elect members of [[Hull City Council]].<ref>{{cite news |date=1 May 2022 |title=Hull's party leaders face off in pre-election debate |work=BBC News |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/uk-england-humber-61271173 |accessdate=1 May 2022}}</ref> This was on the same day as [[2022 United Kingdom local elections|other local elections]].

Following a review of Ward boundaries by the Local Government Boundary Commission for England (LGBCE) the whole council was elected in 2018, the first placed winning candidate at that election was up for re-election in 2022. The [[Labour Party (UK)|Labour Party]] was defending overall control of the council by a single seat after Labour councillor Julia Conner defected to the Liberal Democrats, on 3 March 2022.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/uk-england-leeds-60604712|title=Hull Labour councillor Julia Conner defects to Liberal Democrats|date=3 March 2022|work=BBC News|accessdate=3 March 2022}}</ref> There were no elections in Ings or Kingswood wards, being two member wards and not being on this round of the three-year cycle.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.hull.gov.uk/council-and-democracy/elections-and-voting/local-elections|title=Local Elections|publisher=Hull City Council|accessdate=5 May 2022}}</ref>

The [[Liberal Democrats (UK)|Liberal Democrats]] won control of the council, ending 10 years of Labour administration.<ref>{{cite news |date=6 May 2022 |title=Election results 2022: Hull Lib Dems end Labour's 10-year rule |work=BBC News |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/uk-england-humber-61343655 |accessdate=6 May 2022}}</ref>

